WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:13.682 --> 00:00:15.640
FLAMINIA CATTERUCCIA: I'm Flaminia Catteruccia,

00:00:15.640 --> 00:00:19.190
an associate professor at the Harvard T.H. Chan School of Public Health.

00:00:19.190 --> 00:00:21.890
And today, I'm going to give a lecture on vector biology

00:00:21.890 --> 00:00:24.470
and the dynamics of malaria transmission.

00:00:24.470 --> 00:00:27.110
The lecture is divided into five chapters.

00:00:27.110 --> 00:00:30.710
In the first chapter, I'll talk about the complexity of mosquito biology,

00:00:30.710 --> 00:00:33.200
and how that influences malaria transmission.

00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:36.500
In the second chapter, I'll look at the journey of the Plasmodium parasites

00:00:36.500 --> 00:00:37.820
within the mosquito.

00:00:37.820 --> 00:00:41.275
In the third chapter, I'll introduce the concept of vectorial capacity, which

00:00:41.275 --> 00:00:43.400
is the interaction of the biological processes that

00:00:43.400 --> 00:00:45.170
are important for transmission.

00:00:45.170 --> 00:00:47.750
In chapter 4, I'll discuss current methods that

00:00:47.750 --> 00:00:49.490
are available for mosquito control.

00:00:49.490 --> 00:00:52.034
While in chapter 5, I'll discuss alternative strategies

00:00:52.034 --> 00:00:53.950
that might become available in the near future

00:00:53.950 --> 00:00:56.060
and might take us to malaria eradication.

00:00:56.060 --> 00:00:59.480
Let me start with chapter 1, and describe the complex mosquito biology

00:00:59.480 --> 00:01:02.180
and how that influences malaria transmission.

00:01:02.180 --> 00:01:04.670
Malaria is transmitted to humans exclusively

00:01:04.670 --> 00:01:07.130
by mosquitoes of the Anopheles genus.

00:01:07.130 --> 00:01:10.800
There are about 500 Anopheles species in the world.

00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:15.420
However, only 30 to 40 are considered important vectors of human malaria.

00:01:15.420 --> 00:01:20.090
These species are spread throughout the globe, as seen here in this slide.

00:01:20.090 --> 00:01:24.470
And they have very different behaviors during larval and adult stages.

00:01:24.470 --> 00:01:28.190
During larval development, which lasts about 7 to 10 days,

00:01:28.190 --> 00:01:32.310
mosquito are adapted to very different ecological habitats.

00:01:32.310 --> 00:01:34.365
Some mosquito species for instance, prefer

00:01:34.365 --> 00:01:38.840
breeding in temporary breeding sites, such as those provided by rain puddles.

00:01:38.840 --> 00:01:43.100
While others would prefer breeding in permanent breeding sites, such as rice

00:01:43.100 --> 00:01:44.510
fields.

00:01:44.510 --> 00:01:47.180
Some mosquitoes are highly adapted to salt water,

00:01:47.180 --> 00:01:49.520
and so live in coastal areas.

00:01:49.520 --> 00:01:53.060
While other mosquitoes can only survive in fresh water.

00:01:53.060 --> 00:01:55.910
These different ecological habitats expose mosquitoes

00:01:55.910 --> 00:01:57.140
to different environments.

00:01:57.140 --> 00:01:59.723
For instance, different temperature conditions and temperature

00:01:59.723 --> 00:02:03.920
shifts between day and night, also different microorganisms, predators,

00:02:03.920 --> 00:02:05.330
and nutritional reserves.

00:02:05.330 --> 00:02:07.840
In turn, the ecological habitats to which

00:02:07.840 --> 00:02:10.130
these mosquitoes are exposed as larvae will

00:02:10.130 --> 00:02:13.970
impact the physiology of adults emerging from those breeding sites,

00:02:13.970 --> 00:02:16.220
with possible consequences for malaria transmission.

00:02:16.220 --> 00:02:20.330
For instance, larvae that are adapted to temporary breeding sites,

00:02:20.330 --> 00:02:22.640
might have reduced availability of nutrients

00:02:22.640 --> 00:02:25.070
and might have a shorter developmental time.

00:02:25.070 --> 00:02:27.530
So the adults emerging from those breeding sites

00:02:27.530 --> 00:02:30.410
will generally be smaller in size and might

00:02:30.410 --> 00:02:33.890
need to feed more often as adults, on blood, in order

00:02:33.890 --> 00:02:39.030
to meet the energetic demands of flying, mating, reproducing, and so on.

00:02:39.030 --> 00:02:42.209
This in turn, might create more opportunities for Plasmodium parasites

00:02:42.209 --> 00:02:44.375
for their own transmissions, because a mosquito that

00:02:44.375 --> 00:02:48.170
bites more often obviously will be more capable of transmitting malaria.

00:02:48.170 --> 00:02:50.450
Let's take a look at the mosquito life cycle,

00:02:50.450 --> 00:02:53.790
starting from male and female adults emerging from pupa.

00:02:53.790 --> 00:02:56.090
After emerging, males and females will have

00:02:56.090 --> 00:02:59.420
to find each other in order to mate, so that the female can become inseminated

00:02:59.420 --> 00:03:01.520
with sperm transferred by the male.

00:03:01.520 --> 00:03:05.040
Then the female will have to go and look for a host for a blood meal.

00:03:05.040 --> 00:03:06.920
And after taking a blood meal, she will rest

00:03:06.920 --> 00:03:11.000
for a couple of days, during which time she will digest the blood components

00:03:11.000 --> 00:03:13.940
and incorporate them into the developing eggs.

00:03:13.940 --> 00:03:16.567
After developing the eggs, the female will use the sperm

00:03:16.567 --> 00:03:18.900
that she's received by the male to fertilize those eggs.

00:03:18.900 --> 00:03:22.010
And then she'll lay them in suitable oviposition sites.

00:03:22.010 --> 00:03:24.455
The eggs that are laid will then hatch into larvae.

00:03:24.455 --> 00:03:27.200
And the larvae will undergo four developmental stages

00:03:27.200 --> 00:03:28.940
before transforming into pupa.

00:03:28.940 --> 00:03:31.160
From where then male and female adults will emerge

00:03:31.160 --> 00:03:33.030
and the cycle will continue.

00:03:33.030 --> 00:03:37.140
Blood feeding is really an essential component in the mosquito life cycle.

00:03:37.140 --> 00:03:40.010
And this is a step in which mosquitoes from different species

00:03:40.010 --> 00:03:42.830
exhibit quite a variety of behaviors.

00:03:42.830 --> 00:03:46.830
For instance, some mosquito species are highly adapted to feeding on humans.

00:03:46.830 --> 00:03:48.990
So they are highly anthropophilic.

00:03:48.990 --> 00:03:50.990
While other mosquito species predominantly

00:03:50.990 --> 00:03:55.880
feed on animals, such as birds, or cows, or rats.

00:03:55.880 --> 00:03:58.100
In order to transmit malaria to humans mosquitoes

00:03:58.100 --> 00:04:00.350
will have to have a certain degree of anthropophilia,

00:04:00.350 --> 00:04:03.330
because they will need to feed on humans at least twice.

00:04:03.330 --> 00:04:05.390
The first time they'll have to bite a person that

00:04:05.390 --> 00:04:08.890
is infected with malaria parasites and they'll ingest those parasites.

00:04:08.890 --> 00:04:12.770
And then after a few days, once sporogonic development is completed,

00:04:12.770 --> 00:04:16.850
then that mosquito will inject those parasites into the next human being.

00:04:16.850 --> 00:04:20.209
The propensity of a mosquito to feed on humans, rather than animals,

00:04:20.209 --> 00:04:21.529
can actually be measured.

00:04:21.529 --> 00:04:24.080
And this is the so-called human blood index.

00:04:24.080 --> 00:04:27.710
The human blood index can be measured by collecting mosquitoes in the field

00:04:27.710 --> 00:04:29.510
and looking at the content of their blood

00:04:29.510 --> 00:04:32.360
to see whether it's from animal or from human origin.

00:04:32.360 --> 00:04:34.130
This value goes from 0 to 1.

00:04:34.130 --> 00:04:37.940
With 0, being mosquitoes that feed exclusively on animals, so highly

00:04:37.940 --> 00:04:38.750
zoophilic.

00:04:38.750 --> 00:04:43.664
And 1, with mosquitoes that feed only on humans, so highly anthropophilic.

00:04:43.664 --> 00:04:45.830
As you can see in this map, there's high indigeneity

00:04:45.830 --> 00:04:48.890
among mosquitoes for the human blood index.

00:04:48.890 --> 00:04:52.700
And many mosquito species with a high level of anthropophilia

00:04:52.700 --> 00:04:54.630
actually cluster in Africa.

00:04:54.630 --> 00:04:57.380
And these mosquitoes belong to mosquitoes of the Anopheles gambiae

00:04:57.380 --> 00:05:00.920
complex, such as Anopheles gambiae, Anopheles coluzzii.

00:05:00.920 --> 00:05:04.110
But also, Anopheles funestus, which is another very important vector

00:05:04.110 --> 00:05:05.900
of human malaria in Africa.

00:05:05.900 --> 00:05:07.860
Mosquitoes with a high level of anthropophilia

00:05:07.860 --> 00:05:11.720
are also found in Papua New Guinea, like Anopheles farauti,

00:05:11.720 --> 00:05:14.420
as well as in South America, even if to a lesser extent,

00:05:14.420 --> 00:05:15.924
like Anopheles darlingi.

00:05:15.924 --> 00:05:18.090
In any given area, there might be multiple Anopheles

00:05:18.090 --> 00:05:22.460
species that live in sympatry and this might complicate control measures.

00:05:22.460 --> 00:05:24.350
For instance, let's have a look at Africa,

00:05:24.350 --> 00:05:26.520
and the Anopheles gambiae complex.

00:05:26.520 --> 00:05:29.800
This complex comprises eight mosquito species

00:05:29.800 --> 00:05:31.970
that are morphologically identical and can only be

00:05:31.970 --> 00:05:34.490
distinguished on the molecular level.

00:05:34.490 --> 00:05:37.430
These species include some of the most important malaria vectors,

00:05:37.430 --> 00:05:41.270
such as Anopheles gambiae, Anopheles arabiensis, and Anopheles coluzzii.

00:05:41.270 --> 00:05:45.110
As well as some more minor vectors, like Anopheles melas and merus,

00:05:45.110 --> 00:05:47.300
which are adapted to coastal areas, because they

00:05:47.300 --> 00:05:50.060
prefer breathing in salt water.

00:05:50.060 --> 00:05:52.220
Also the complex includes some species that

00:05:52.220 --> 00:05:54.740
are not considered vectors of human malaria,

00:05:54.740 --> 00:05:56.900
such as Anopheles quadriannulatus, because they

00:05:56.900 --> 00:05:59.302
tend to bite predominately on animals.

00:05:59.302 --> 00:06:01.010
If you look at the major malaria vectors,

00:06:01.010 --> 00:06:04.160
Anopheles gambiae and Anopheles coluzzii, these until recently,

00:06:04.160 --> 00:06:08.200
were believed to be the same species and just different molecular forms.

00:06:08.200 --> 00:06:12.230
Anopheles gambiae was previously known as Anopheles gambiae S form.

00:06:12.230 --> 00:06:16.575
While Anopheles coluzzii was previously known as Anopheles gambiae M form.

00:06:16.575 --> 00:06:19.700
Anopheles gambiae and Anopheles coluzzii have very similar biting behavior,

00:06:19.700 --> 00:06:24.110
because they both tend to feed on humans and late at night and indoors.

00:06:24.110 --> 00:06:26.770
However, they show quite different ecological habitats

00:06:26.770 --> 00:06:28.550
due to larval developments.

00:06:28.550 --> 00:06:31.880
Anopheles gambiae tend to prefer living in temporary breeding sites,

00:06:31.880 --> 00:06:33.180
such as rain puddles.

00:06:33.180 --> 00:06:36.810
While Anopheles coluzzii prefer living in permanent breeding sites,

00:06:36.810 --> 00:06:38.960
such as rice fields.

00:06:38.960 --> 00:06:42.230
We don't know yet, whether these two species contribute differently

00:06:42.230 --> 00:06:43.540
to malaria transmission.

00:06:43.540 --> 00:06:46.220
Anopheles arabiensis is a third important malaria

00:06:46.220 --> 00:06:49.400
vector that shares habitats with Anopheles gambiae and Anopheles

00:06:49.400 --> 00:06:50.540
coluzzii.

00:06:50.540 --> 00:06:52.670
Until recently, this mosquito species was not

00:06:52.670 --> 00:06:55.460
considered to be a very important vector for human malaria,

00:06:55.460 --> 00:06:58.610
because these mosquitoes also feed on animals, apart from humans,

00:06:58.610 --> 00:07:01.640
and so they dilute potential infectious bites.

00:07:01.640 --> 00:07:04.220
However, because of their plastic behavior,

00:07:04.220 --> 00:07:07.380
and the fact that these mosquitoes can feed outdoors and rest outdoors,

00:07:07.380 --> 00:07:09.950
apart from feeding and resting indoors, these mosquitoes

00:07:09.950 --> 00:07:13.460
become more difficult to be controlled with current vector control strategies.

00:07:13.460 --> 00:07:16.040
And so in recent years, they've become more and more relevant

00:07:16.040 --> 00:07:17.410
for malaria transmission.

00:07:17.410 --> 00:07:19.760
When multiple mosquito species live in sympatry,

00:07:19.760 --> 00:07:22.790
it becomes really difficult to understand the relative contribution

00:07:22.790 --> 00:07:24.240
to malaria transmission.

00:07:24.240 --> 00:07:29.030
This is well explained in the study by Fontenille et al., conducted in 1997,

00:07:29.030 --> 00:07:31.990
in a small village in Senegal, called Dielmo.

00:07:31.990 --> 00:07:35.210
What the authors did, they looked at the relative contribution

00:07:35.210 --> 00:07:37.820
of three important malaria vectors, Anopheles gambiae,

00:07:37.820 --> 00:07:40.340
Anopheles arabiensis, and Anopheles funestus,

00:07:40.340 --> 00:07:42.200
over the course of three years.

00:07:42.200 --> 00:07:44.270
It is important to note that at that time

00:07:44.270 --> 00:07:47.060
Anopheles gambiae were considered to be a single species.

00:07:47.060 --> 00:07:50.260
Well, now we know that those species contain both Anopheles gambiae

00:07:50.260 --> 00:07:52.370
and Anopheles coluzzii.

00:07:52.370 --> 00:07:55.520
What the authors did is they collected mosquitoes from this village

00:07:55.520 --> 00:07:57.560
and then they analyzed their salivary glands

00:07:57.560 --> 00:08:00.767
to look for the presence of sporozoites of Plasmodium falciparum.

00:08:00.767 --> 00:08:02.850
And after this analysis, what they could determine

00:08:02.850 --> 00:08:06.440
is that Anopheles gambiae mosquitoes were a very important vector in year

00:08:06.440 --> 00:08:09.510
one, while instead the contribution declined in year two,

00:08:09.510 --> 00:08:11.860
and was negligible in year three.

00:08:11.860 --> 00:08:15.260
While Anopheles arabiensis had almost the opposite pattern.

00:08:15.260 --> 00:08:17.930
They were a negligible vector in year one, a little bit more

00:08:17.930 --> 00:08:21.620
important in year two, and much more relevant in year three.

00:08:21.620 --> 00:08:24.700
While Anopheles funestus had an intermediate pattern.

00:08:24.700 --> 00:08:28.280
And they were an important vector in year one, also during the dry season

00:08:28.280 --> 00:08:31.400
because these mosquitoes are better adapted to dry conditions,

00:08:31.400 --> 00:08:35.870
they disappeared during year two, and they came back strong in year three.

00:08:35.870 --> 00:08:38.929
This study is a good example of the complexity of malaria transmission,

00:08:38.929 --> 00:08:42.530
even in a small village and prior to any vector control

00:08:42.530 --> 00:08:44.540
intervention, as it was back then.

00:08:44.540 --> 00:08:48.110
And also shows importance of performing longitudinal studies,

00:08:48.110 --> 00:08:50.990
across a number of years, looking at all possible mosquito

00:08:50.990 --> 00:08:54.800
species that might be contributing to malaria transmission.

